      "math_academy_id": "q-73584",
      "knowledge_point_id": "cd47a43f-1e99-5909-aa48-da8690025197",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Using Standard Gaussian Elimination with Row Exchanges",
      "problem": "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then solve the linear system.",
      "worked_solution": "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we get ${a}_{22}=0.$ Therefore, we swap the $2$nd and $3$rd rows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y-2z=3 \\\\ y+5z=-8 \\\\ z=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The third equation $z=4$ tells us the value of $z.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+5(4)=-8\\,⟹\\,y=-28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $y=-28$ and $z=4$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+(-28)-2(4)=3\\,⟹\\,x=39.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution is $x=39$, $y=-28$, and $z=4.$ Or, in vector form $⟨39,-28,4⟩.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
